+AcquireIQ--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+AWS Deployment with TerraformSetup guide covering the AWS services used, how they connect, the one-time setup, and the automated GitHub Actions deployment.Status Draft. The Terraform files and the deploy-to-aws.yml workflow have not yet been run through terraform plan or against a real AWS account. Treat every step as untested until it has been run.1. OverviewAcquireIQ has three parts. A React frontend built with Vite, a Node.js and Express backend packaged as a Docker image listening on port 3002, and a PostgreSQL database. The backend also calls OpenAI for natural-language search.Terraform creates the AWS infrastructure. The GitHub Actions workflow deploy-to-aws.yml runs Terraform and then ships the app, so after a one-time setup every deployment is a single click with no manual steps. It is separate from deploy-to-vps.yml, which deploys to the Linode VPS.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ Design goal  One public HTTPS address. The browser loads the frontend and calls the API on the same domain, so there are no mixed-content or CORS problems and the backend is never exposed directly to the internet.2. Architecture2.1 Architecture diagramSolid lines numbered 1 to 6 are a visitor's request. Dashed lines lettered A to E are a deployment. Colours follow a colour-blind-safe palette, one per service category, with a legend on the right. The API and database each run in one Availability Zone that AWS chooses; the diagram shows one possible placement.2.2 Request flow Browser    |  HTTPS    v CloudFront  (https://<id>.cloudfront.net)    |-- /acquireiq/*             --> S3 bucket (React build, private)    |-- /api/*  /requests*       --> Application Load Balancer (HTTP :80)    |-- /dashboard*  /assist/*         |                                       v                               ECS Fargate task (API :3002)                                       |                                       v                               RDS PostgreSQL 17 (:5432, private)2.3 Network layoutThe app gets its own VPC across two Availability Zones (AZs), each zone being a separate AWS data center in us-east-1.PartAddress rangeWhat is in itAcquireIQ VPC10.20.0.0/16Everything that needs its own network address.Public subnets (one per zone)10.20.0.0/24, 10.20.1.0/24Routed to the internet gateway. Hold the load balancer and, by default, the API and db-init tasks with public IPs. The tasks are still only reachable from the load balancer.Private subnets (one per zone)10.20.10.0/24, 10.20.11.0/24No route from the internet. Hold the database. The second private subnet is required by RDS and is spare unless Multi-AZ is turned on or AWS moves the database there.Internet gatewayn/aThe VPC's door to the internet, used by the public subnets.NAT gateway (optional)n/aOff by default. Set use_nat_gateway = true to move the API and db-init tasks into the private subnets with no public IP. Adds an hourly charge.S3, ECR, Secrets Manager, CloudWatch Logs and IAM sit outside the VPC because they are shared regional services run by AWS. The tasks call them over HTTPS, and IAM permissions, not network rules, decide what they may do. CloudFront runs at AWS edge locations worldwide.2.4 Network securityEach layer accepts traffic only from the layer directly in front of it.Security groupAllows inFromacquireiq-albTCP 80CloudFront origin-facing IP ranges only (AWS managed prefix list)acquireiq-ecsTCP 3002The load balancer security groupacquireiq-dbTCP 5432The ECS security group (API and db-init tasks)2.5 Health checks and recovery   1. The load balancer calls /api/health on the API task every 30 seconds.   2. Two failures in a row mark the task unhealthy, and ECS stops it and starts a replacement.   3. With one task there is a gap of a minute or two. API calls fail with HTTP 503, the React app retries each call twice, then shows Backend unavailable. The page itself still loads from S3.   4. Five passing checks in a row mark the new task healthy, and traffic resumes.All of this is automatic. Running two tasks (desired_count = 2) would remove the gap at roughly double the Fargate cost.3. AWS Services UsedServiceTerraform resourcesPurposeAmazon VPCVPC, 2 public and 2 private subnets, internet gateway, route tables, optional NAT gatewayThe app's own network. Public subnets for the load balancer and API, private subnets for the database. See section 2.3.Amazon S3frontend bucket (main), state bucket (bootstrap)Frontend bucket stores the React build, readable only by CloudFront. State bucket stores Terraform state, versioned and encrypted.Amazon CloudFrontaws_cloudfront_distribution, origin access controlHTTPS entry point. Serves static files from S3 and forwards API paths to the load balancer with caching off.Elastic Load Balancingaws_lb, target group, listenerApplication Load Balancer that routes requests to the backend and health-checks /api/health.Amazon ECRtwo repositories (bootstrap)Private registries for the backend image and the db-init image.Amazon ECS on Fargatecluster, service, two task definitionsRuns the backend container (0.25 vCPU, 512 MB) and the one-off db-init task, with no EC2 instances to manage.Amazon RDS for PostgreSQLaws_db_instance, subnet groupPostgreSQL 17 on db.t4g.micro, 20 GB, not publicly accessible.AWS Secrets Managerfour secretsDATABASE_URL, OPENAI_API_KEY, DB_MASTER_PASSWORD, DB_APP_PASSWORD. ECS injects them into containers at start.AWS IAMtask execution role, GitHub OIDC provider and deploy role (bootstrap)ECS role pulls images, writes logs and reads secrets. Deploy role lets GitHub Actions log in to AWS without stored keys.Amazon CloudWatch Logstwo log groupsBackend and db-init container output, kept for 14 days.4. Files in the RepositoryFilePurposeinfra/bootstrap/main.tfRun once from your PC. Creates the Terraform state bucket, the two ECR repositories, and the GitHub OIDC login role.infra/main.tfAll app infrastructure. Applied by the workflow on every deploy. State is stored in the bootstrap bucket.infra/db-init/DockerfileSmall PostgreSQL client image containing setup-database.sql.infra/db-init/init.shCreates the app role with the real password, then runs setup-database.sql. Must keep LF line endings..github/workflows/deploy-to-aws.ymlThe automated deployment.5. How a Deployment WorksEach run of deploy-to-aws.yml does the following with no manual steps.   5. Logs in to AWS through GitHub OIDC. No AWS keys are stored in GitHub.   6. Builds the backend and db-init Docker images and pushes them to ECR, tagged with the git commit SHA.   7. Runs terraform apply, creating or updating all infrastructure and pointing ECS at the new images.   8. Runs the db-init task inside the VPC to create or update the database schema. The deploy fails if it fails.   9. Waits for the backend service to become stable.   10. Builds the frontend, uploads it to S3 under acquireiq/, and clears the CloudFront cache.   11. Checks /api/health through CloudFront and prints the app address.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ Trigger  The workflow runs only when started by hand from the Actions tab, so it never runs alongside deploy-to-vps.yml on a push. To deploy on every push to main, add a push trigger as described in the workflow's header comment.6. PrerequisitesNeeded only on your PC for the one-time bootstrap. Regular deployments run entirely in GitHub.   oo An AWS account and an IAM user or SSO role with administrator rights.   oo Terraform 1.10 or later. The configuration pins the AWS provider to version ~> 6.0.   oo AWS CLI v2, configured with aws configure or aws configure sso.7. One-Time Setup7.1 Keep Terraform files out of GitAdd these lines to the repository's .gitignore before running Terraform..gitignore infra/**/.terraform/ infra/**/*.tfstate* infra/**/*.tfvars7.2 Run the bootstrapWindows PowerShell, from the repository root cd infra\bootstrap terraform init terraform applyKeep the local terraform.tfstate this creates in infra\bootstrap. It holds no secrets but is needed to change or remove the bootstrap resources later.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ If apply fails on the OIDC provider  An AWS account can have only one GitHub OIDC provider. If one already exists, import it with terraform import aws_iam_openid_connect_provider.github <its ARN> and apply again.7.3 Add GitHub secretsIn GitHub, open Settings, then Secrets and variables, then Actions, and add these repository secrets.SecretValueAWS_ROLE_ARNBootstrap output github_deploy_role_arnTF_STATE_BUCKETBootstrap output tf_state_bucketDB_MASTER_PASSWORDA strong password, letters and digits onlyDB_APP_PASSWORDA strong password, letters and digits only. It is placed inside the DATABASE_URL connection string, so symbols could break it.OPENAI_API_KEYYour OpenAI API key7.4 Run the first deploymentIn GitHub, open the Actions tab, choose Deploy to AWS, then Run workflow. The first run takes longer because RDS and CloudFront each take several minutes to create. The last step prints the app address.8. Database SetupRDS is private, so GitHub's runners cannot reach it. Instead the workflow starts the db-init image as a one-off ECS task inside the VPC. Each run it does three things.   12. Creates the acquireiq_app role if missing and sets its password to DB_APP_PASSWORD. Because the role already exists, setup-database.sql skips its hardcoded local password.   13. Grants the RDS postgres admin the right to act as acquireiq_app. RDS's admin is not a full superuser, and PostgreSQL 16 and later require this for CREATE DATABASE ... OWNER and SET ROLE.   14. Runs setup-database.sql, which only creates objects that are missing, so it is safe on every deploy.Demo seed data is not loaded automatically. Output from each run is in the /ecs/acquireiq-db-init CloudWatch log group.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ Untested  This sequence mirrors what code-checks.yml does against a disposable PostgreSQL container, but it has not yet been run against RDS.9. Running Terraform by HandOptional. Useful for terraform plan before a deploy or for troubleshooting. Terraform prompts for the three secret variables, or reads them from TF_VAR_* environment variables.Windows PowerShell, from the repository root cd infra $bucket = terraform -chdir=bootstrap output -raw tf_state_bucket terraform init -backend-config="bucket=$bucket" terraform plan10. Known Gaps Before Production--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ Two major deficiencies, accepted for this demo  No API authentication: anyone with the URL can read, create and change acquisition requests and record approvals. No rate limiting: anyone can flood the API, including the search endpoint that calls OpenAI. Set a monthly spending limit on the OpenAI key. Both must be added before the app holds real data.AreaCurrent stateRecommended changeAPI authenticationNone. No login, session, token or API key checkAdd authentication before any real dataRate limitingNone, in code or in AWSAdd rate limiting in the API or AWS WAF on CloudFront; set an OpenAI spending limit nowDeploy roleAdministratorAccessLimit to the services main.tf actually managesDomainDefault *.cloudfront.net addressCustom domain, see section 13DatabaseSingle AZ, skip_final_snapshot = trueMulti-AZ, deletion protection, final snapshot, backupsDatabase TLSEncrypted, certificate not verified (sslmode=no-verify)Verify against the AWS RDS CA bundleAPI networkingTasks in public subnets with public IPs (default)Set use_nat_gateway = true, or add VPC endpoints, to move them into private subnetsApprovalWorkflow applies without reviewRun terraform plan on pull requests and require approval before apply11. CostThe always-on charges are the Application Load Balancer, the Fargate task, and the RDS instance. A rough estimate is $40 to $60 per month in us-east-1 at idle. This figure is unverified. Confirm it with the AWS Pricing Calculator before relying on it.12. Tear DownThe workflow never deletes anything. To remove the app infrastructure, run terraform destroy by hand as in section 9. Terraform asks for the three secret variables, and any values work for a destroy. This permanently deletes the database and its data with no final snapshot.The bootstrap resources stay in place. The state bucket has prevent_destroy set, so removing it also requires deleting that line from infra/bootstrap/main.tf first.13. Using Your Own DomainAfter the first deploy, the app is reachable at https://<id>.cloudfront.net/acquireiq/, where <id> is a random name AWS assigns. That address is for testing only. Visitors should use a leelinkoff.com address instead.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ Key constraint  DNS maps a hostname to a server. It cannot map a path. A request to leelinkoff.com/mvps/acquireiq always reaches whatever server leelinkoff.com points to, which is currently the Linode running Apache (unverified, confirm before relying on it). So the URL choice decides where the traffic enters.13.1 OptionsOptionWhat visitors typeHow it worksTrade-offs1. Linode forwards to AWSleelinkoff.com/mvps/acquireiqApache on Linode proxies the path to the CloudFront address.Every request passes through Linode, then AWS. If Linode is down, the app is down. Needs frontend and Terraform path changes.2. Subdomain on AWSacquireiq.leelinkoff.comA GoDaddy CNAME points the subdomain straight at CloudFront.Recommended for AWS. Linode is not involved and app code is unchanged. Not a /mvps/ path.3. Host on Linode, no AWSleelinkoff.com/mvps/acquireiqdeploy-to-vps.yml deploys the container and frontend to Linode, like the RAG app.No AWS cost. Needs PostgreSQL on Linode and the workflow fixes still pending.13.2 Option 2 setup: acquireiq.leelinkoff.com   15. Request an ACM certificate for acquireiq.leelinkoff.com in us-east-1. CloudFront only accepts certificates from that region. Choose DNS validation.   16. Add the validation CNAME record that ACM shows to the leelinkoff.com DNS zone at GoDaddy, then wait for the certificate status to show Issued.   17. Add the domain and certificate to the CloudFront distribution in infra/main.tf, replacing the existing viewer_certificate block, then run the deploy workflow.infra/main.tf, inside aws_cloudfront_distribution aliases = ["acquireiq.leelinkoff.com"]   viewer_certificate {   acm_certificate_arn      = "<certificate ARN from step 1>"   ssl_support_method       = "sni-only"   minimum_protocol_version = "TLSv1.2_2021" }   18. At GoDaddy, add a CNAME record with host acquireiq pointing to the CloudFront domain (the <id>.cloudfront.net part of app_url).   19. Open https://acquireiq.leelinkoff.com/acquireiq/ and confirm the app loads with a valid certificate.13.3 Remove the /acquireiq/ suffixWith a dedicated subdomain, the app can live at the root so visitors type only acquireiq.leelinkoff.com.WhereChangefrontend/vite.config.jsChange base: '/acquireiq/' to base: '/'.infra/main.tfAdd default_root_object = "index.html" to aws_cloudfront_distribution, and change the app_url output to end in /.deploy-to-aws.ymlUpload to the bucket root (s3://.../ instead of s3://.../acquireiq/), invalidate /*, and adjust the health check URL.13.4 Redirect the raw CloudFront addressThe <id>.cloudfront.net address keeps working after the custom domain is added. A CloudFront Function attached to the default cache behavior as a viewer-request function sends anyone using it to the real domain.CloudFront Function (JavaScript) function handler(event) {   var host = event.request.headers.host.value;   if (host !== 'acquireiq.leelinkoff.com') {     return {       statusCode: 301,       statusDescription: 'Moved Permanently',       headers: { location: { value: 'https://acquireiq.leelinkoff.com' + event.request.uri } }     };   }   return event.request; }Attach it to the default behavior only. Redirecting the API behaviors would break POST and PATCH requests.13.5 Option 1 notes: leelinkoff.com/mvps/acquireiq through LinodeIf the exact /mvps/acquireiq path is required while still running on AWS, all of these change together.   oo frontend/vite.config.js base becomes /mvps/acquireiq/, and the frontend is built with VITE_API_BASE=/mvps/acquireiq so API calls stay under the proxied path.   oo The S3 upload prefix and the CloudFront path patterns change to match the new paths.   oo Apache on Linode gets a ProxyPass for /mvps/acquireiq/ to the CloudFront address, with ProxyPreserveHost Off so CloudFront receives its own hostname.   oo Optionally, Header unset lines in Apache remove the Via, X-Cache and Server headers that reveal CloudFront and S3.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ Recommendation  If the exact leelinkoff.com/mvps/acquireiq URL matters most, Option 3 delivers it with the least moving parts. If AWS is the goal, use Option 2 with sections 13.3 and 13.4.
