+<div class="band" style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap">
+  <h2>Why some services sit outside the VPC</h2>
+  <p style="max-width:70ch;margin:0 0 12px">The VPC is the app's private network. Only things that need their own IP address in it go inside. Everything else is a service AWS runs itself and the app calls over HTTPS.</p>
+  <div class="scroll" style="border:0;margin:0"><table class="stack"><thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>Services</th><th>Why there, and what controls access</th></tr></thead><tbody>
+  <tr><td data-l="Where">Inside the VPC</td><td data-l="Services">Load balancer, API task, db-init task, RDS</td><td data-l="Why there, and what controls access">Each gets an IP address in the app's subnets. Security groups and route tables decide who can reach them.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td data-l="Where">Region, outside the VPC</td><td data-l="Services">S3, ECR, Secrets Manager, CloudWatch Logs, IAM</td><td data-l="Why there, and what controls access">Shared regional services run by AWS. They are not placed anywhere; the app calls them over HTTPS through the internet gateway. Access is controlled by IAM permissions, not network rules.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td data-l="Where">Global edge</td><td data-l="Services">CloudFront</td><td data-l="Why there, and what controls access">Runs in AWS edge locations worldwide, close to visitors. It reads the S3 bucket because the bucket policy allows only this distribution, and reaches the load balancer at its public address.</td></tr>
+  </tbody></table></div>
+  <p class="note">Optional VPC endpoints would give the VPC a private path to S3, ECR, Secrets Manager and CloudWatch so that traffic never crosses the internet. They are mainly useful with private subnets and no NAT gateway, and most have an hourly charge. Not needed for this demo.</p>
+</div></div>
+
+<div class="band" style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap">
+  <h2>Why there is a load balancer with only one API task</h2>
+  <p style="max-width:70ch;margin:0 0 12px">With one API task the load balancer spreads nothing. Its job here is to give CloudFront one name that never changes, while the API's address changes every time ECS starts a new task. Addresses below are illustrative.</p>
+  <div class="scroll" style="border:0;margin:0"><table class="stack"><thead><tr><th>When</th><th>API task address</th><th>What CloudFront points at</th></tr></thead><tbody>
+  <tr><td data-l="When">Monday, first deploy</td><td data-l="API task address"><code>10.20.0.37</code> (public <code>3.91.44.12</code>)</td><td data-l="What CloudFront points at"><code>acquireiq-1234567890.us-east-1.elb.amazonaws.com</code></td></tr>
+  <tr><td data-l="When">Wednesday, after a crash or new deploy</td><td data-l="API task address"><code>10.20.1.112</code> (public <code>54.210.8.77</code>). The old address is gone.</td><td data-l="What CloudFront points at">The same load balancer name. Nothing to update.</td></tr>
+  </tbody></table></div>
+  <h3>Who does what</h3>
+  <div class="scroll" style="border:0;margin:0"><table class="stack"><thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Does what</th><th>Configured by</th></tr></thead><tbody>
+  <tr><td data-l="Who">AWS Fargate</td><td data-l="Does what">Gives each new API task a new IP address. Nobody picks it.</td><td data-l="Configured by">Automatic</td></tr>
+  <tr><td data-l="Who">ECS service</td><td data-l="Does what">Adds a new task's IP to the load balancer's target list when it starts, and removes the old IP when that task stops.</td><td data-l="Configured by"><code>load_balancer</code> block in <code>aws_ecs_service.backend</code></td></tr>
+  <tr><td data-l="Who">Load balancer</td><td data-l="Does what">Sends each request to whichever listed IP is passing health checks. It never changes any IP itself, and its own name stays fixed.</td><td data-l="Configured by"><code>aws_lb</code>, <code>aws_lb_target_group</code></td></tr>
+  <tr><td data-l="Who">CloudFront</td><td data-l="Does what">Always sends API calls to the load balancer's fixed name.</td><td data-l="Configured by"><code>domain_name = aws_lb.main.dns_name</code></td></tr>
+  </tbody></table></div>
+  <p class="note">Without a load balancer, CloudFront would point straight at the API's IP, and every crash or deploy would break the app until CloudFront was updated by hand. The load balancer also gives health checks and deploys without downtime. Its classic job, spreading load across several copies, starts automatically if <code>desired_count</code> is raised above 1.</p>
+</div></div>
